 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Victorian women's job market has softened over the past year. Employment is roughly flat, but unemployment is up. All figures below are seasonally adjusted, for September 2026.\n\n**Victorian women, September 2026**\n- **Unemployment rate:** 5.10%, up from 4.16% in July 2025. It peaked at 5.24% in July 2026.\n- **Participation rate:** 63.6%, down from 63.9% in July 2025.\n- **Employment-to-population ratio:** 60.4%, down from 61.2% in July 2025. It bottomed at 59.5% in April 2026.\n- **Employed women:** about 1.843 million, up about 13,000 since July 2025. Employment fell to about 1.803 million in April 2026 and has since recovered.\n- **Full-time employment:** about 1.008 million, down about 29,000 since July 2025. Almost all of the net job growth has been part-time, though I didn't pull the part-time figures directly.\n\n**Comparisons**\n- **Australian women:** the national female unemployment rate is 4.61%, so Victorian women are about 0.5 percentage points higher.\n- **Victorian men:** their unemployment rate is 5.01%, essentially unchanged over the year (5.01% in July 2025). Women's unemployment has risen faster than men's. It is now slightly above men's, having been below it a year ago.\n- **Participation:** Victorian men's participation rate is 71.5%, well above women's.\n\n**Overall:** The labour market for women in Victoria has weakened since early 2026. Unemployment rose from about 4.1% to about 5.1%, and the number of full-time jobs fell. Employment has partly recovered since April. Australia-wide unemployment for women has also been rising, from 4.09% to 4.61%, so this isn't only a Victorian trend. Monthly state figures by sex are noisy, so I'd treat movements of a few tenths of a percentage point with caution.",
